\description{
Density, distribution function, random generation, and \code{brms} custom family
for the Shifted Wald distribution, also known as the Shifted Inverse Gaussian.
A Wald-distributed decision time is shifted by a non-decision time \code{ndt}, and
a fixed proportion \code{poutlier} of responses is generated by an outlier process
instead of by the decision process. The drift rate can be fixed across trials
(the classic Wald) or drawn afresh on each one, with SD \code{sigmadrift}, and
the non-decision time can be fixed or spread over a range \code{sigmandt}.

Functions:
\itemize{
\item \code{rcogmod_invgaussian()}: Simulates random draws.
\item \code{dcogmod_invgaussian()}: Computes the density (likelihood).
\item \code{pcogmod_invgaussian()}: Computes the cumulative distribution function (CDF).
\item \code{cogmod_invgaussian()}: Creates a \code{brms::custom_family()}.
\item \code{cogmod_invgaussian_stanvars()}: Generates the \code{stanvars} to pass to \code{brm()}.
}
}
\details{
The Wald distribution describes the time it takes for a Wiener diffusion
process starting at 0 to reach a threshold \code{boundary} > 0, given a positive drift
rate \code{drift} > 0. That time is then shifted by a non-decision time \code{ndt}.

It is mathematically equivalent to shifting an Inverse Gaussian distribution
with mean \code{boundary / drift} and shape \code{boundary^2}. In the \code{brms} family the drift rate
is named \code{mu}, since \code{brms} requires a parameter of that name.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds rather than as a fraction of the fastest
observed response, what the half Normal outlier component is for, and why
the outlier component's scale is a constant rather than a \code{dpar}, and why
reaction times have to be in seconds.

The Wald density vanishes at the shift with all derivatives, like the
LogNormal, so \code{ndt} is well behaved here and there is no unbounded-likelihood
boundary of the kind \code{\link[=cogmod_gamma]{cogmod_gamma()}} and \code{\link[=cogmod_weibull]{cogmod_weibull()}} have at shape \verb{< 1}.

The random generation algorithm is that of Michael, Schucany, and Haas
(1976), as used in the \code{statmod} package.
}
\section{Across-trial drift variability}{
\code{sigmadrift} is the between-trial SD of the drift rate. At \code{sigmadrift = 0},
its default, every trial accumulates at the same rate and the model is the
classic Wald. Above zero, each trial draws its own drift from a
\code{Normal(mu, sigmadrift)} \strong{truncated at zero}, which is what lets the model
produce the long right tails empirical RT distributions have, and is the
single-accumulator counterpart of what \code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}} calls \code{sigmadrift} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_lba1]{cogmod_lba1()}} calls \code{sigma}. Marginalising over that draw is a Gaussian
integral, so the density stays closed form and costs two normal CDFs.

The truncation matters. A single-boundary accumulator handed a negative drift
never terminates, so an untruncated Normal would leave the density defective:
it integrates to 0.99 at \verb{mu = 3, boundary = 0.5, sigmadrift = 1.5}, and to
0.69 at \verb{mu = 0.5, boundary = 1, sigmadrift = 2}. \code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}} needs no
such truncation, a diffusion between two boundaries always absorbing at one
of them.

In a formula, \code{sigmadrift = 0} \strong{fixes} the parameter and gives the classic
Wald; leaving it out of \code{bf()} altogether \emph{estimates} it, which is not the
same thing:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Classic Wald
brms::bf(rt ~ 1, boundary ~ 1, sigmadrift = 0, sigmandt = 0, ndt ~ 1,
         poutlier ~ 1, family = cogmod_invgaussian())

# With across-trial drift variability
brms::bf(rt ~ 1, boundary ~ 1, sigmadrift ~ 1, sigmandt = 0, ndt ~ 1,
         poutlier ~ 1, family = cogmod_invgaussian())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Fixing it is the better default, for two reasons. \code{sigmadrift} and
\code{poutlier} both fatten the right tail and are only weakly distinguishable:
on 2000 simulated trials at \verb{mu = 3, boundary = 0.5, sigmadrift = 0.8},
estimating \code{sigmadrift} buys about 2 log-likelihood units over fixing it at
zero, and the outlier weight absorbs most of the difference. And scaling
\code{mu}, \code{boundary} and \code{sigmadrift} up by a common factor sends the Wald to
the reciprocal-normal (LATER) limit, where the within-trial noise stops
mattering and \code{RT = boundary / drift} exactly - so large values of all three
describe nearly the same distribution. \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} gives \code{sigmadrift}
a deliberately informative prior to fence off both.

One consequence is worth stating plainly: with \code{sigmadrift > 0} the density
decays as \code{t^-2}, because drifts arbitrarily close to zero take arbitrarily
long, so \strong{the mean does not exist}. \link[brms:posterior_epred.brmsfit]{posterior_epred()}
returns \code{Inf}, as it does for \code{\link[=cogmod_invgamma]{cogmod_invgamma()}} at \code{shape <= 1}. Use
\link[brms:posterior_predict.brmsfit]{posterior_predict()} and summarise the draws with a
median or a quantile instead.
}

\section{Across-trial non-decision time variability}{
\code{sigmandt} is the between-trial \emph{range} of the non-decision time, \code{st0} in
the usual notation, and the single-boundary counterpart of \code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}}'s
\code{sigmandt}. At \code{sigmandt = 0}, its default, every trial has the same
non-decision time \code{ndt}. Above zero each trial draws its own from
\code{Uniform(ndt, ndt + sigmandt)}, so \code{ndt} becomes the \strong{lower bound} of the
non-decision time and the leading edge of the distribution is spread over an
interval instead of starting sharply. Averaging the shift out turns the
density into a difference of two Wald CDFs and the CDF into a difference of
two integrated CDFs, and for the Wald both are closed form, so the parameter
costs a handful of normal CDFs per observation and \code{cens()} works with it
unchanged. With \code{sigmadrift > 0} as well, everything is taken by the same
quadrature over the drift that the CDF already uses.

\strong{It is hard to estimate, and for most applications should be fixed at
zero.} Three parameters shape the leading edge of the distribution: \code{ndt}
places it, \code{sigmandt} smears it and \code{poutlier} puts mass in front of it, and
they trade off against one another on any dataset without a sharp onset.
\code{st0} is also the parameter the DDM literature agrees is recovered worst.
Estimate it only with a lot of data, a strong prior, or both -
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} gives it the same deliberately tight prior as
\code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}}'s - and otherwise write \code{sigmandt = 0} in \code{bf()}, which
removes it from the model altogether. As with \code{sigmadrift}, leaving it out
of \code{bf()} \emph{estimates} it.
}

\section{Censoring: errors, timeouts and omissions}{
\code{brms}'s \code{cens()} addition term works on this family:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{brms::bf(rt | cens(error) ~ condition, boundary ~ condition, ndt ~ 1,
         sigmadrift = 0, sigmandt = 0, family = cogmod_invgaussian())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A trial with \code{error = 1} (or \code{TRUE}, or \code{"right"}) is then a
\strong{right-censored} correct response: its RT is read as a lower bound on when
the correct process would have finished, and it contributes the survival
\code{P(T > rt)} to the likelihood where an observed response contributes the
density. With \code{sigmadrift = 0} this is the \emph{simple} censored shifted Wald of
Miller et al. (2018, their Eq. 4), the \code{version = "simple"} of the \code{cswald}
model in the \code{bmm} package. Miller et al. also give a \emph{competing-risks}
variant (their Eq. 5): a race between two Wald accumulators with drifts \code{v}
and \code{-v}, each response scored with its own defective density. That is the
model implemented in \code{rtdists} and in \code{bmm}'s \code{version = "crisk"}, and it is
a choice model of the same kind as \code{\link[=cogmod_rdm]{cogmod_rdm()}}, not a censoring
construction - \code{cens()} does not fit it. Where it is wanted, \code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}}
with \code{bias} fixed at 0.5 covers the same ground. Here censoring is not a
separate family but a construction: no new parameter, no new syntax, and the
same \code{cens()} works on every RT-only family with a closed-form CDF (see
\code{?rcogmod_lognormal}). Left-censoring (\code{error = -1} or \code{"left"}) and
interval-censoring (\code{cens(x, y2)}) work the same way, and \code{log_lik()} -
hence \code{loo()} - honours all three.

Three things follow from the construction:
\itemize{
\item \strong{What it is for.} Timeouts, deadlines, omissions and go/no-go designs,
where a non-response genuinely is a censored draw from one accumulator and
the censoring time says nothing about the process beyond "not finished
yet". There the construction is exact - not an approximation - and it uses
the censored trials instead of discarding them; the example below shows
what discarding them costs. A two-accumulator race, by contrast, has to
estimate an error process, and when errors are few that process is
identified by nothing (see the \code{driftone} discussion in \code{\link[=cogmod_rdm]{cogmod_rdm()}});
censoring has no error accumulator to run away.
\item \strong{What it assumes.} Non-informative censoring: that an error says nothing
about the correct process beyond "not finished yet". That holds when the
error comes from a process independent of the correct one - a second
accumulator, a lapse, a deadline - and fails when both responses come from
one evidence path, which is the DDM's picture. Under an unbiased DDM the
error RTs have the \emph{same} distribution as the correct ones, so an error
censors a trial that was as likely fast as slow, and the survival scores
it wrong. The bias is not small: in simulation, fitting this construction
to DDM-generated data with 5\% errors overestimates the drift by about 15\%
and the boundary by about 10\%, and with 10\% errors by about 30\% and 15\%,
which is why Miller et al. argue for the simple censored Wald only above
roughly 95\% accuracy. \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} warns past 20\% censored trials,
the threshold \code{bmm} uses, but for commission errors the model is on firm
ground only well below that.
\item \strong{The check to run first.} Censoring draws errors from the surviving
tail, so it can only ever predict them \emph{slower} than correct responses.
Compare the two RT distributions before fitting. Errors clearly slower
than correct responses are consistent with the construction; errors as
fast as correct responses are the signature of a single diffusion process,
and faster errors that of a low boundary or a biased start point. In both
of the latter cases use a race that models the error response:
\code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}}, \code{\link[=cogmod_rdm]{cogmod_rdm()}} or \code{\link[=cogmod_lba2]{cogmod_lba2()}}.
}

\code{posterior_predict()} predicts the latent, uncensored reaction time, as
\code{brms} does for its own families, so \code{pp_check()} on a censored fit compares
uncensored replicates against data whose censored rows hold \emph{censoring}
times. \code{pcogmod_invgaussian(lower.tail = FALSE)} is the survival a censored
trial contributes, and the Stan \code{cogmod_invgaussian_lccdf()} in
\code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian_stanvars]{cogmod_invgaussian_stanvars()}} is its counterpart; with \code{sigmadrift > 0}
both take the CDF by quadrature over the drift, the marginal having no
closed form. \code{sigmandt > 0} changes neither: the smeared CDF and survival
are closed form at a fixed drift and go through the same quadrature
otherwise.
}

\examples{
# Simulate 1000 RTs with 2\% outliers
rts <- rcogmod_invgaussian(1000, drift = 3, boundary = 0.5, ndt = 0.2, poutlier = 0.02)
hist(rts, breaks = 50, xlab = "RT (s)")

# The same, with the drift varying across trials: a longer right tail
rts_sv <- rcogmod_invgaussian(1000, drift = 3, boundary = 0.5, ndt = 0.2,
                              sigmadrift = 1, poutlier = 0.02)
quantile(rts, c(0.5, 0.99))
quantile(rts_sv, c(0.5, 0.99))

# Responses faster than ndt keep positive density, unlike the unmixed model
dcogmod_invgaussian(0.1, ndt = 0.3, poutlier = 0.02)
dcogmod_invgaussian(0.1, ndt = 0.3, poutlier = 0)

# A non-decision time spread over [0.2, 0.3] s smears the leading edge
x <- seq(0.2, 0.5, by = 0.05)
rbind(fixed = dcogmod_invgaussian(x, ndt = 0.2),
      spread = dcogmod_invgaussian(x, ndt = 0.2, sigmandt = 0.1))

# Censoring, Miller et al.'s (2018) demonstration in miniature. A deadline
# at 1.2 s turns the slowest responses into omissions. Fitted by maximum
# likelihood three ways: dropping those trials, keeping the deadline as if
# it were the RT, and right-censoring them at the deadline - which is what
# `cens()` does in brms. Only the last recovers the generating parameters.
set.seed(1)
rt <- rcogmod_invgaussian(2000, drift = 2, boundary = 1, ndt = 0.3, poutlier = 0)
deadline <- 1.2
censored <- rt > deadline
y <- pmin(rt, deadline)
mean(censored)  # about 12\% of the trials

nll <- function(par, how) {
  drift <- exp(par[1]); boundary <- exp(par[2]); ndt <- min(y) * plogis(par[3])
  ld <- dcogmod_invgaussian(y, drift, boundary, ndt, poutlier = 0, log = TRUE)
  ls <- pcogmod_invgaussian(y, drift, boundary, ndt, poutlier = 0,
                            lower.tail = FALSE, log.p = TRUE)
  -switch(how,
    drop   = sum(ld[!censored]),
    keep   = sum(ld),
    censor = sum(ld[!censored]) + sum(ls[censored]))
}
fit <- function(how) {
  o <- optim(c(0, 0, log(4)), nll, how = how)
  c(drift = exp(o$par[1]), boundary = exp(o$par[2]), ndt = min(y) * plogis(o$par[3]))
}
round(rbind(truth = c(2, 1, 0.3), drop = fit("drop"), keep = fit("keep"),
            censor = fit("censor")), 2)

}
